1
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:09,820
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

2
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:14,780
"Lord Alcinous, most renowned among all peoples,
truly it is a fine thing to hear a singer

3
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:18,340
such as this man is, with a voice like the gods'.

4
00:00:18,340 --> 00:00:23,480
For I say there is no accomplishment more
graceful than when joy holds a whole people,

5
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:26,620
and feasters up and down the
hall sit listening to a singer,

6
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:32,180
seated in order, and beside them the tables are loaded
with bread and meats, and the cupbearer draws wine

7
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:35,500
from the bowl and carries it round and fills the cups:

8
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:38,180
this seems to my mind the fairest thing there is.

9
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:44,060
But your heart has turned to asking after my grievous
cares — so that I may mourn and groan the more.

10
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:47,140
What first, then, what last shall I tell through?

11
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:50,880
For the gods of heaven have
given me sorrows past counting.

12
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:57,280
Now first I will tell my name, so that you too may know it —
and I hereafter, if I escape the pitiless day,

13
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:00,540
may be your guest-friend, though my home is far away.

14
00:01:00,540 --> 00:01:07,640
I am Odysseus, son of Laertes — known to all mankind
for my cunning designs; and my fame has reached heaven.

15
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:10,460
My home is Ithaca, clear-skied under its mountain,

16
00:01:10,460 --> 00:01:16,860
Neriton, tall in its shivering leaves; and around
it islands lie in a cluster, close to one another —

17
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:19,880
Dulichium and Same and wooded Zacynthus.

18
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:26,740
Ithaca itself lies low, farthest out in the sea toward
the dark of sunset — the rest face dawn and the sun —

19
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:29,360
rough ground, but a good nurse of men. I cannot

20
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:33,280
see any sweeter sight, for my part,
than a man's own country.

21
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:36,080
The queenly goddess Calypso would
have kept me there beside her

22
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:42,620
in her hollow caves, hungry to make me her husband,
and so, in the same way, Circe detained me in her halls,

23
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:46,000
the guileful lady of Aeaea, hungry to make me her husband:

24
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,880
but they never once persuaded the heart in my breast.

25
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:52,060
So true it is that nothing is
sweeter than a man's own country

26
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:58,640
and his own parents, even if he lives in a rich house
far away in a strange land, cut off from his parents.

27
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:02,480
But come — let me tell you also my
journey home, and all its cares,

28
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:05,020
which Zeus laid on me on the way from Troy.

29
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:11,020
From Ilium the wind bore me to the Cicones — to Ismarus.
There I sacked the city and destroyed the men;

30
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:13,480
and out of the city we took their wives and much substance

31
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:17,440
and divided all, so that no man
might go cheated of his share by me.

32
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:22,280
Then, you may be sure, I ordered a quick-footed retreat —
and they, the great fools, would not obey.

33
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:25,720
There and then much wine was being drunk, and many sheep

34
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:29,360
they slaughtered along the shore,
and shambling crook-horned cattle;

35
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:35,440
and meanwhile Cicones went crying to Cicones,
their neighbors inland — more of them, and better men,

36
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:39,440
skilled at fighting men from chariots and,
where need was, on foot.

37
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:43,040
They came, then, thick as leaves
and flowers come in their season,

38
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:49,460
in the morning mist. Then an evil doom of Zeus stood by us,
luckless men, to load us with pains in plenty.

39
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:52,920
They set their battle-line and fought by the fast ships,

40
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:55,820
and each side pelted each with bronze-tipped spears.

41
00:02:55,820 --> 00:02:59,180
As long as it was morning,
and the sacred day still growing,

42
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:02,760
so long we stood them off and held
our ground, though they were more;

43
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:08,960
but when the sun turned over toward unyoking-time,
then the Cicones broke the Achaeans, and mastered them.

44
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:14,840
Six from each ship, six strong-greaved companions
were killed; the rest of us fled from death and doom.

45
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:22,280
From there we sailed on further, grieved at heart,
glad to be out of death, having lost our dear companions.

46
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:28,600
But my curved ships would not go forward before we had
called three times on each of those poor companions

47
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:32,160
who died on the plain, cut down by the Cicones.

48
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:35,880
And Zeus who gathers the clouds roused
the North Wind against the ships

49
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:42,120
in a howling squall, and covered land and sea
alike with cloud; and night rose up out of heaven.

50
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:45,160
The ships were swept along slantwise, their sails

51
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:48,840
ripped into three and four rags
by the strength of the wind.

52
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:54,660
We lowered them into the hulls, in terror of death,
and rowed the ships hard in toward the shore.

53
00:03:54,660 --> 00:03:56,960
There for two nights and two days without a break

54
00:03:56,960 --> 00:04:01,220
we lay, eating our hearts with
weariness and pain together.

55
00:04:01,220 --> 00:04:04,060
But when Dawn of the lovely braids
brought the third day round,

56
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:07,400
we set up the masts and hauled the white sails open

57
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:11,360
and sat, and the wind and the
steersmen held them on course.

58
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:17,700
And now I would have come unharmed to my own country,
but the wave and the current, as I rounded Malea,

59
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:22,000
and the North Wind beat me off
course and drove me wide of Cythera.

60
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,860
From there for nine days I was carried by ruinous winds

61
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:28,560
across the fish-filled sea;
and on the tenth we stepped ashore

62
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:32,540
in the land of the Lotus-eaters,
who eat a flower for food.

63
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:35,320
There we set foot on the mainland and drew water,

64
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:38,980
and the companions quickly took
their meal beside the fast ships.

65
00:04:38,980 --> 00:04:43,680
And when we had tasted food and drink,
then I sent companions ahead to learn

66
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:46,660
what men they were who ate bread upon this earth:

67
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:50,160
two men I chose, and sent a third with them as herald.

68
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:53,740
They went their way at once,
and fell in with the Lotus-eater men;

69
00:04:53,740 --> 00:04:59,300
and the Lotus-eaters had no thought of killing our
companions — but they gave them lotus to taste.

70
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:05,540
And whoever of them ate the honey-sweet fruit of the
lotus no longer wished to bring back word or to return:

71
00:05:05,540 --> 00:05:12,240
there they wanted to stay, among the Lotus-eater men,
browsing the lotus, and to forget their homecoming.

72
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:14,800
These men I forced back weeping to the ships,

73
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:17,740
dragged them under the benches of
the hollow ships and bound them,

74
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:22,240
and I ordered the rest of my trusted
companions to hurry and board the fast ships —

75
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:26,220
for fear some other might eat of
the lotus and forget his homecoming.

76
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:29,600
And they came aboard at once and
took their places at the oarlocks,

77
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:33,400
and sitting in order they struck
the gray salt water with their oars.

78
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:36,320
From there we sailed on further, grieved at heart.

79
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:40,020
And we came to the land of the Cyclopes — the overweening,

80
00:05:40,020 --> 00:05:45,480
the lawless — who, trusting in the deathless gods,
plant no crop with their hands, and plow nothing:

81
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:50,780
everything springs up for them unsown,
unplowed, wheat and barley, and vines that carry

82
00:05:50,780 --> 00:05:54,480
wine in their heavy clusters,
and Zeus' rain swells it for them.

83
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:59,880
They have no counsel-bearing assemblies, no established laws,
but live on the crests of the high mountains

84
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:03,280
in hollow caves, and each man lays down the law

85
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:06,820
to his own wives and children —
and they care nothing for one another.

86
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:14,060
Now a scrubby island stretches outside the harbor there,
neither close in to the Cyclopes' land nor far out,

87
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:21,040
wooded; and on it goats past counting breed,
wild goats: no tread of men scares them away,

88
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:26,720
no hunters push through to it, men who suffer hard
days in the timber, working the mountain crests;

89
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:32,700
it is held by no flocks and no plowed fields,
but unsown and unplowed all its days

90
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:36,600
the island is widowed of men,
and feeds its bleating goats.

91
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:42,020
For the Cyclopes have no red-cheeked ships,
and no shipwrights among them who might labor out

92
00:06:42,020 --> 00:06:47,160
well-benched vessels to accomplish every need,
crossing to the cities of the world, the way men

93
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:50,560
so often traverse the sea in ships to one another:

94
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:53,980
such craftsmen would have made
the island a fair settlement too.

95
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:58,440
For it is not a bad island at all:
it would bear every crop in season.

96
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:01,780
There are meadows in it along the
shores of the gray salt water,

97
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:08,520
soft, well-watered; vines there would never die;
there is level plowland, and a deep standing harvest always

98
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:12,380
ready for the scythe in season,
for the soil beneath is rich.

99
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:16,800
And there is a harbor with safe anchorage,
where there is no need of mooring-line —

100
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:20,740
no anchor-stones to throw, no stern-cables to make fast:

101
00:07:20,740 --> 00:07:25,660
you beach her, and wait until the sailors'
hearts prompt them, and the breezes blow.

102
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:31,860
And at the harbor's head runs shining water,
a spring under a cave, with black poplars growing round it.

103
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:38,060
There we sailed in — and some god was guiding us —
through the dead of night, with nothing to be seen ahead:

104
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:43,820
a fog lay thick around the ships, and the moon
showed nothing from heaven, shut in by clouds.

105
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:49,080
No one's eyes made out that island then, nor did we
see the long combers rolling in against the shore

106
00:07:49,080 --> 00:07:51,900
before the well-benched ships ran up the beach.

107
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:55,240
And with the ships beached, we took down all the sails

108
00:07:55,240 --> 00:07:58,120
and stepped out ourselves onto the surf-line of the sea,

109
00:07:58,120 --> 00:08:01,740
and there we dropped asleep, and waited for shining Dawn.

110
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:08,740
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,
we walked that island marveling, all through it.

111
00:08:08,740 --> 00:08:11,840
And the nymphs, daughters of aegis-bearing Zeus, started

112
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:15,680
the mountain goats,
so my companions might have their meal.

113
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:19,300
At once we fetched our curved
bows and long-socketed goat-spears

114
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:21,800
out of the ships, and forming in three bands

115
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:26,340
we shot; and quickly the god gave
us game to our hearts' content.

116
00:08:26,340 --> 00:08:32,660
Twelve ships followed with me, and to each nine
goats fell by lot; for me alone they picked out ten.

117
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:39,120
So then all day long till the setting of the sun we
sat feasting on meat past counting and sweet wine.

118
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:41,700
For the red wine was not from out our ships:

119
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:44,660
there was still some left,
for each crew had drawn off much

120
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:48,420
in two-handled jars when we
took the Cicones' holy citadel.

121
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:52,020
And we looked across at the land
of the Cyclopes, who were near:

122
00:08:52,020 --> 00:08:55,400
smoke, and voices — theirs, and their sheep's and goats'.

123
00:08:55,400 --> 00:09:00,980
And when the sun went down and the dusk came on,
then we lay down to sleep on the surf-line of the sea.

124
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:04,360
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

125
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:07,460
then I called an assembly, and spoke among them all:

126
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:12,900
'The rest of you stay here now, my trusted companions,
while I with my own ship and my own crew

127
00:09:12,900 --> 00:09:16,280
go and make trial of those men over there — what they are:

128
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:22,680
whether violent, savage, without justice,
or kind to strangers, with hearts that fear the gods.'

129
00:09:22,680 --> 00:09:28,880
So speaking I went aboard, and ordered my companions
to board in their turn and loose the stern-cables.

130
00:09:28,880 --> 00:09:32,320
And they came aboard at once and
took their places at the oarlocks,

131
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:36,000
and sitting in order they struck
the gray salt water with their oars.

132
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:42,460
But when we reached the place — it was not far —
there on the land's edge, close by the sea, we saw a cave,

133
00:09:42,460 --> 00:09:48,960
high, overhung with laurels. There many flocks —
sheep and goats — were penned at night; around it a yard

134
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:54,960
ran high-built, of stones set deep in the earth,
and tall pines, and oaks with lofty crowns.

135
00:09:54,960 --> 00:10:00,960
And there a man slept, a monster of a man, who shepherded
his flocks alone and far apart; he kept no company

136
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:04,680
with others, but held aloof, and his heart knew no law.

137
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:09,860
For he was built a monstrous wonder — nothing
like a man who eats bread, but like a wooded crag

138
00:10:09,860 --> 00:10:13,200
of the high mountains, standing clear of all the rest.

139
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:18,580
Then I ordered the rest of my trusted companions
to stay there by the ship and guard the ship,

140
00:10:18,580 --> 00:10:24,100
while I picked the twelve best of my companions
and went. And I took a goatskin of dark wine,

141
00:10:24,100 --> 00:10:30,780
sweet wine, that Maron gave me, Euanthes' son,
the priest of Apollo, the god who watches over Ismarus —

142
00:10:30,780 --> 00:10:34,040
because we had protected him, with his child and his wife,

143
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:39,620
out of reverence: for he lived in the wooded grove
of Phoebus Apollo. And he gave me shining gifts:

144
00:10:39,620 --> 00:10:44,940
he gave me seven talents of well-wrought gold,
and he gave me a mixing-bowl all of silver, and then

145
00:10:44,940 --> 00:10:51,220
wine, drawn off in two-handled jars, twelve in all,
sweet and unmixed, a drink for gods. Not one

146
00:10:51,220 --> 00:10:54,280
of the slaves or handmaids in his house knew of it —

147
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:58,360
only himself and his dear wife, and one housekeeper only.

148
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:03,600
Whenever they drank that honey-sweet red wine,
he would fill one cup and pour it into twenty measures

149
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:10,120
of water, and a sweet smell would rise from the bowl,
a marvel: no joy then in holding back.

150
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:13,300
Of this I filled a great skin and
brought it, and provisions too

151
00:11:13,300 --> 00:11:17,040
in a sack: for my proud heart had a foreboding, even then,

152
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:19,980
that a man would come against
me clothed in great strength,

153
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:23,480
a savage, knowing nothing of judgments or of laws.

154
00:11:23,480 --> 00:11:30,020
Quickly we came to the cave, and did not find him within:
he was herding his fat flocks in the pasture.

155
00:11:30,020 --> 00:11:32,920
We went into the cave and stared in wonder at everything:

156
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:40,160
crates sagging with cheeses; pens crowded with
lambs and kids — each sort folded separately,

157
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:46,940
the firstlings apart, apart the middle-born,
apart again the newborn; and every vessel swimming with whey,

158
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:51,000
the pails and bowls, all made
by hand, that he milked into.

159
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,080
There first my companions begged me, pleading:

160
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:59,000
take some cheeses and go back; then quickly,
driving the kids and lambs out of their pens

161
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,730
down to the fast ship, sail away over the salt water.

162
00:12:02,730 --> 00:12:05,910
But I would not listen — it would have been far better —

163
00:12:05,910 --> 00:12:09,370
not until I saw him, and whether
he would give me guest-gifts.

164
00:12:09,370 --> 00:12:12,310
And his appearing was to be no joy to my companions.

165
00:12:12,310 --> 00:12:16,390
We lit a fire there and made an
offering, and helped ourselves

166
00:12:16,390 --> 00:12:23,690
to cheeses, and ate, and sat inside waiting for him,
until he came back herding. He carried a huge weight

167
00:12:23,690 --> 00:12:29,790
of dry wood, to serve him at supper-time,
and flung it down inside the cave with a crash;

168
00:12:29,790 --> 00:12:33,370
and we in terror shrank away into the cave's far corner.

169
00:12:33,370 --> 00:12:38,950
He drove his fat flocks into the wide cavern —
all those he milked; the males he left outside,

170
00:12:38,950 --> 00:12:42,290
the rams and he-goats, out in the deep yard.

171
00:12:42,290 --> 00:12:48,270
Then he lifted high and set in place a huge door-stone,
a massive thing: twenty-two carts, good ones,

172
00:12:48,270 --> 00:12:51,310
four-wheeled, could not have levered it off the ground —

173
00:12:51,310 --> 00:12:54,430
such a towering cliff of rock he set in the doorway.

174
00:12:54,430 --> 00:13:01,190
Then, sitting, he milked the ewes and bleating goats,
all in due order, and set the young under each.

175
00:13:01,190 --> 00:13:06,370
And straightway he curdled half the white milk and
gathered it and stored it in the plaited baskets,

176
00:13:06,370 --> 00:13:11,730
and half he stood in vessels, so it might be there for
him to take and drink, and serve him at supper-time.

177
00:13:11,730 --> 00:13:17,610
And when at last he had hurried through his work,
then he lit the fire — and saw us, and asked:

178
00:13:17,610 --> 00:13:21,410
'Strangers, who are you?
From where do you sail the watery ways?

179
00:13:21,410 --> 00:13:26,290
Is it on some business — or do you rove at
random, the way sea-raiders do, who wander

180
00:13:26,290 --> 00:13:30,070
staking their own lives, bearing evil to strangers?'

181
00:13:30,070 --> 00:13:36,790
So he spoke, and our own hearts broke within us in
terror of the deep voice and the monster himself.

182
00:13:36,790 --> 00:13:39,130
Even so I answered him, and said:

183
00:13:39,130 --> 00:13:45,150
'We are Achaeans, blown off course from Troy by every
wind there is, across the great gulf of the sea,

184
00:13:45,150 --> 00:13:52,070
making for home — but by other routes, other roads
we came: so Zeus, I suppose, chose to devise it.

185
00:13:52,070 --> 00:13:55,390
We declare ourselves the people of Agamemnon, Atreus' son,

186
00:13:55,390 --> 00:14:00,310
whose fame is now the greatest thing under heaven,
so vast the city he sacked, so many the peoples

187
00:14:00,310 --> 00:14:03,850
he destroyed. But we, for our part,
have come to your knees

188
00:14:03,850 --> 00:14:06,590
in hope you may offer guest-friendship — or give besides

189
00:14:06,590 --> 00:14:10,470
some gift, as is the right and custom done for strangers.

190
00:14:10,470 --> 00:14:13,650
Respect the gods, mighty one. We are your suppliants,

191
00:14:13,650 --> 00:14:16,610
and Zeus is the avenger of suppliants and of strangers —

192
00:14:16,610 --> 00:14:20,330
Zeus of Strangers, who walks beside honored guests.'

193
00:14:20,330 --> 00:14:23,950
So I spoke, and he answered me
at once, with pitiless heart:

194
00:14:23,950 --> 00:14:27,370
'You are a fool, stranger —
or you have come from far away —

195
00:14:27,370 --> 00:14:30,530
you who tell me to fear the gods or shun their anger.

196
00:14:30,530 --> 00:14:33,810
The Cyclopes pay no heed to Zeus who bears the aegis,

197
00:14:33,810 --> 00:14:37,210
nor to the blessed gods: for we are the better men by far.

198
00:14:37,210 --> 00:14:43,390
I would not spare you or your companions to dodge
the hatred of Zeus — not unless my own heart bade me.

199
00:14:43,390 --> 00:14:46,670
But tell me: where did you moor
your well-made ship when you came?

200
00:14:46,670 --> 00:14:50,590
At the far end of the shore, or near? I want to know.'

201
00:14:50,590 --> 00:14:57,150
So he spoke, testing; but I knew too much, and he did
not catch me; I answered him back with words of guile:

202
00:14:57,150 --> 00:15:00,710
'My ship — Poseidon the
earth-shaker smashed her to pieces,

203
00:15:00,710 --> 00:15:03,330
driving her on the rocks at the border of your country:

204
00:15:03,330 --> 00:15:06,270
and the wind carried her in from the sea.

205
00:15:06,270 --> 00:15:09,590
But I, with these men here, escaped sheer destruction.'

206
00:15:09,590 --> 00:15:13,410
So I spoke; and out of his
pitiless heart he answered nothing,

207
00:15:13,410 --> 00:15:16,570
but sprang up and reached his hands out at my companions,

208
00:15:16,570 --> 00:15:19,170
snatched up two together, and dashed them like puppies

209
00:15:19,170 --> 00:15:23,370
against the ground: their brains
ran out on the earth, and wetted it.

210
00:15:23,370 --> 00:15:26,230
He tore them limb from limb and made ready his supper,

211
00:15:26,230 --> 00:15:29,490
and he ate like a mountain-bred lion, and left nothing —

212
00:15:29,490 --> 00:15:33,150
the entrails, the flesh, the bones with their marrow.

213
00:15:33,150 --> 00:15:39,730
And we, weeping, lifted up our hands to Zeus at the
horror we watched, helplessness holding our hearts.

214
00:15:39,730 --> 00:15:45,990
And when the Cyclops had filled his great belly,
feeding on man-flesh and washing it down with unmixed milk,

215
00:15:45,990 --> 00:15:49,230
he lay stretched out in the cave among his sheep.

216
00:15:49,230 --> 00:15:56,350
And I made a plan in my own great heart to steal close,
draw the sharp sword from beside my thigh,

217
00:15:56,350 --> 00:15:59,910
and stab him in the chest,
where the midriff holds the liver,

218
00:15:59,910 --> 00:16:03,990
feeling for the place with my hand.
But a second thought held me:

219
00:16:03,990 --> 00:16:09,730
there we too would have perished in sheer destruction,
for we could never have pushed back with our hands

220
00:16:09,730 --> 00:16:13,090
the massive stone he had set in the high doorway.

221
00:16:13,090 --> 00:16:16,430
So then, groaning, we waited for shining Dawn.

222
00:16:16,430 --> 00:16:20,090
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

223
00:16:20,090 --> 00:16:27,130
then he lit the fire, and milked his famous flocks,
all in due order, and set the young under each.

224
00:16:27,130 --> 00:16:29,350
And when at last he had hurried through his work,

225
00:16:29,350 --> 00:16:33,430
he snatched up two men together
again, and made his meal ready.

226
00:16:33,430 --> 00:16:37,210
And having fed, he drove his fat flocks out of the cave,

227
00:16:37,210 --> 00:16:43,890
lifting off the great door-stone with ease — and then
set it back, the way a man sets the lid on a quiver.

228
00:16:43,890 --> 00:16:46,830
With a great whistling the Cyclops turned his fat flocks

229
00:16:46,830 --> 00:16:51,230
toward the mountain; and I was left
brooding evil deep in my heart:

230
00:16:51,230 --> 00:16:55,510
if somehow I might pay him back,
and Athena grant me the glory.

231
00:16:55,510 --> 00:16:59,770
And this, as I turned it over, seemed to me the best plan.

232
00:16:59,770 --> 00:17:05,490
There lay beside a pen a great club of the Cyclops',
green olive-wood: he had cut it to carry

233
00:17:05,490 --> 00:17:11,470
once it seasoned. Looking it over we judged it
big as the mast of a black twenty-oared ship,

234
00:17:11,470 --> 00:17:15,150
a broad-beamed freighter, that crosses the great gulf:

235
00:17:15,150 --> 00:17:18,470
that was its length, that its thickness to the eye.

236
00:17:18,470 --> 00:17:21,890
Standing to it, I cut off about a fathom's measure

237
00:17:21,890 --> 00:17:25,530
and handed it to my companions,
and told them to dress it down;

238
00:17:25,530 --> 00:17:28,970
and they made it smooth,
while standing by I brought it to a point

239
00:17:28,970 --> 00:17:33,310
at the tip, and then took it and
hardened it in the blazing fire.

240
00:17:33,310 --> 00:17:36,350
And I laid it carefully by, hiding it under the dung

241
00:17:36,350 --> 00:17:39,930
that was strewn in a great deep litter through the cave.

242
00:17:39,930 --> 00:17:45,170
And I told the others to shake lots among them for
who would dare, together with me, to lift the stake

243
00:17:45,170 --> 00:17:48,930
and grind it in his eye, when sweet sleep came on him.

244
00:17:48,930 --> 00:17:51,610
The lot fell on the very men I would have chosen —

245
00:17:51,610 --> 00:17:55,810
four of them, and I counted
myself in with them, the fifth.

246
00:17:55,810 --> 00:17:59,230
At evening he came back, herding his fair-fleeced flocks,

247
00:17:59,230 --> 00:18:02,210
and drove those fat flocks straight into the wide cavern,

248
00:18:02,210 --> 00:18:06,110
all of them: he left not one outside in the deep yard,

249
00:18:06,110 --> 00:18:09,470
whether some foreboding moved him, or a god so ordered it.

250
00:18:09,470 --> 00:18:12,770
Then he lifted high and set in place the great door-stone,

251
00:18:12,770 --> 00:18:18,670
and sitting, he milked the ewes and bleating goats,
all in due order, and set the young under each.

252
00:18:18,670 --> 00:18:20,970
And when at last he had hurried through his work,

253
00:18:20,970 --> 00:18:25,210
he snatched up two men together again,
and made his supper ready.

254
00:18:25,210 --> 00:18:31,910
And then I came up close and spoke to the Cyclops,
holding in my hands an ivy-wood bowl of the dark wine:

255
00:18:31,910 --> 00:18:35,550
'Cyclops — here, drink wine,
now you have fed on man-flesh:

256
00:18:35,550 --> 00:18:37,450
so you may know what kind of drink our ship

257
00:18:37,450 --> 00:18:41,450
carried stored. I was bringing it
as an offering for you, in hope

258
00:18:41,450 --> 00:18:46,050
you might pity me and send me home.
But your madness is past all bearing.

259
00:18:46,050 --> 00:18:48,610
Merciless one — how will any other man of all the many

260
00:18:48,610 --> 00:18:52,870
ever come near you again?
What you have done is past all measure.'

261
00:18:52,870 --> 00:18:57,690
So I spoke; and he took it, and drank it down;
and it pleased him — terribly —

262
00:18:57,690 --> 00:19:00,850
the sweet drink; and he demanded a second helping:

263
00:19:00,850 --> 00:19:03,910
'Give me more — be generous. And tell me your name,

264
00:19:03,910 --> 00:19:07,690
here, now, so I can give you a
guest-gift that will make you glad.

265
00:19:07,690 --> 00:19:11,150
The grain-giving plowland bears
wine for the Cyclopes too —

266
00:19:11,150 --> 00:19:19,590
fine clusters, and Zeus' rain swells them for us;
but this — this is a rivulet of ambrosia and nectar!'

267
00:19:19,590 --> 00:19:23,090
So he spoke, and I handed him the fire-bright wine again.

268
00:19:23,090 --> 00:19:27,930
Three times I brought and gave it;
three times, in his folly, he drained it.

269
00:19:27,930 --> 00:19:33,910
And when the wine had come circling round the Cyclops' wits,
then I spoke to him with honeyed words:

270
00:19:33,910 --> 00:19:40,210
'Cyclops, you ask my glorious name? Then I will tell it —
and you give me the guest-gift, as you promised.

271
00:19:40,210 --> 00:19:46,470
Nobody is my name. Nobody is what they call me —
my mother and my father and all my companions.'

272
00:19:46,470 --> 00:19:50,150
So I spoke, and he answered me
at once, with pitiless heart:

273
00:19:50,150 --> 00:19:56,510
'Nobody I will eat last of all his company,
and the others before him. That shall be your guest-gift.'

274
00:19:56,510 --> 00:20:02,470
He spoke — and reeling back he fell flat, and lay there
with his thick neck lolled over sideways; and sleep

275
00:20:02,470 --> 00:20:06,590
the all-subduer took him. And from his gullet gushed wine

276
00:20:06,590 --> 00:20:11,050
and gobbets of human flesh,
as he belched in his drunkenness.

277
00:20:11,050 --> 00:20:16,410
And then I drove the stake under the deep ash-heap
to heat; and with my words I put courage in all

278
00:20:16,410 --> 00:20:20,950
my companions, so no man might flinch and hang back.

279
00:20:20,950 --> 00:20:24,050
But when that olive stake in
the fire, green though it was,

280
00:20:24,050 --> 00:20:28,070
was on the point of catching,
and glowed clean through, terrible —

281
00:20:28,070 --> 00:20:30,910
then I brought it close out of the fire, and my companions

282
00:20:30,910 --> 00:20:34,890
stood round me; and a great
power breathed courage into us.

283
00:20:34,890 --> 00:20:37,990
They took up the olive stake, sharpened at its point,

284
00:20:37,990 --> 00:20:41,810
and rammed it in his eye; and I,
throwing my weight on from above,

285
00:20:41,810 --> 00:20:47,050
spun it — the way a man bores a ship-timber with
a drill, and his mates below keep it running

286
00:20:47,050 --> 00:20:51,610
with a strap they hold at either end,
and it spins on without stopping:

287
00:20:51,610 --> 00:20:57,790
so we took the fire-pointed stake and spun it in his eye,
and the blood came boiling up round its heat,

288
00:20:57,790 --> 00:21:00,670
and the blast singed all his lids and brows away

289
00:21:00,670 --> 00:21:04,490
as the eyeball burned, and the
roots of it crackled in the fire.

290
00:21:04,490 --> 00:21:10,550
As when a smith dips a great axe-head or an
adze in cold water, and it hisses loud and high,

291
00:21:10,550 --> 00:21:15,730
tempering it — for that is what gives iron its strength —
so his eye hissed round the olive stake.

292
00:21:15,730 --> 00:21:19,530
And he let out a huge and hideous howl,
and the rock rang round it,

293
00:21:19,530 --> 00:21:25,130
and we shrank back in terror. He wrenched the stake
out of his eye, all fouled with streaming blood,

294
00:21:25,130 --> 00:21:30,990
and flung it from him, flailing his arms in agony,
and set up a great bellowing to the Cyclopes who lived

295
00:21:30,990 --> 00:21:33,790
around him in caves along the windy heights.

296
00:21:33,790 --> 00:21:37,070
And they heard his cry and came thronging from every side,

297
00:21:37,070 --> 00:21:40,570
and standing round the cave they asked him what was wrong:

298
00:21:40,570 --> 00:21:43,910
'What in the world, Polyphemus?
What hurt, that you cry out so

299
00:21:43,910 --> 00:21:46,690
through the ambrosial night and make us sleepless?

300
00:21:46,690 --> 00:21:50,230
Surely no mortal is driving off
your flocks against your will?

301
00:21:50,230 --> 00:21:53,750
Surely no one is killing you — by cunning or by force?'

302
00:21:53,750 --> 00:21:56,630
And out of the cave strong Polyphemus answered them:

303
00:21:56,630 --> 00:22:00,290
'Friends — Nobody is killing me,
by cunning and not by force!'

304
00:22:00,290 --> 00:22:02,930
And they answered with winged words, and gave it back:

305
00:22:02,930 --> 00:22:06,150
'If nobody is forcing you, then — alone as you are —

306
00:22:06,150 --> 00:22:09,990
it must be sickness from great Zeus,
and there is no escaping that.

307
00:22:09,990 --> 00:22:12,670
Pray to our father, the lord Poseidon.'

308
00:22:12,670 --> 00:22:15,590
So they spoke as they went; and my own heart laughed,

309
00:22:15,590 --> 00:22:20,370
how my name had utterly deceived him —
the name, and the flawless cunning.

310
00:22:20,370 --> 00:22:23,390
But the Cyclops, groaning and heaving in his agonies,

311
00:22:23,390 --> 00:22:26,810
groped with his hands and lifted
the stone from the doorway,

312
00:22:26,810 --> 00:22:29,870
and sat himself down in the door with both arms spread,

313
00:22:29,870 --> 00:22:33,430
in hope of catching anyone who
tried to slip out with the sheep:

314
00:22:33,430 --> 00:22:36,110
such a fool, I suppose, he hoped to find in me.

315
00:22:36,110 --> 00:22:38,850
But I was planning how it might all come out best —

316
00:22:38,850 --> 00:22:45,110
if I could find some release from death for my companions
and for myself; and I wove every guile and design,

317
00:22:45,110 --> 00:22:49,210
as a man does for his very life:
for the great evil was close.

318
00:22:49,210 --> 00:22:52,810
And this, as I turned it over, seemed to me the best plan.

319
00:22:52,810 --> 00:22:59,990
There were rams there, well-fed, thick in the fleece,
fine big animals, their wool dark as violets.

320
00:22:59,990 --> 00:23:03,630
These, working quietly, I lashed
together with plaited withies —

321
00:23:03,630 --> 00:23:07,330
the ones that monster slept on, the outlaw — in threes:

322
00:23:07,330 --> 00:23:13,310
the middle one of each three carried a man, and the
other two walked either side, protecting my companions.

323
00:23:13,310 --> 00:23:19,830
So three sheep to each man. But for myself —
there was one ram, far the finest of all the flock;

324
00:23:19,830 --> 00:23:22,970
I caught him along the back and
curled under his shaggy belly

325
00:23:22,970 --> 00:23:30,110
and lay there; and in that wondrous fleece, with my
hands twisted fast, I hung on, with an enduring heart.

326
00:23:30,110 --> 00:23:33,290
So then, groaning, we waited for shining Dawn.

327
00:23:33,290 --> 00:23:40,610
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,
then the male flocks poured out toward pasture,

328
00:23:40,610 --> 00:23:46,170
and the unmilked females bleated about the pens with
their udders bursting. And their lord, worn down

329
00:23:46,170 --> 00:23:49,650
by evil agonies, ran his hands
along the backs of all his sheep

330
00:23:49,650 --> 00:23:53,290
as they stood up straight — and never sensed it, the fool:

331
00:23:53,290 --> 00:23:56,730
my men bound under the breasts of his own fleecy sheep.

332
00:23:56,730 --> 00:23:59,910
Last of the flock the great ram walked toward the doorway,

333
00:23:59,910 --> 00:24:03,590
heavy with his wool — and with me,
and my crowding thoughts.

334
00:24:03,590 --> 00:24:07,470
And strong Polyphemus, running his hands over him, said:

335
00:24:07,470 --> 00:24:11,050
'Old sweetheart — my ram — why last of all the flock

336
00:24:11,050 --> 00:24:14,930
through the cave this time? Before,
you were never one to lag behind:

337
00:24:14,930 --> 00:24:21,690
first, always first, striding out to graze the soft
flowering grass, first to reach the running rivers,

338
00:24:21,690 --> 00:24:27,910
first in your longing to turn home to the fold at
evening. And now — last of all. Grieving, are you,

339
00:24:27,910 --> 00:24:30,790
for your master's eye, that a coward of a man put out,

340
00:24:30,790 --> 00:24:34,710
with his miserable companions,
when he had crushed my wits with wine —

341
00:24:34,710 --> 00:24:38,770
Nobody — who is not yet, I promise, clear of destruction.

342
00:24:38,770 --> 00:24:44,190
If only you could think with me, if only you had a
voice to say where that man is dodging from my fury:

343
00:24:44,190 --> 00:24:47,050
then his brains would be dashed
against the floor and spattered

344
00:24:47,050 --> 00:24:49,930
this way and that through the cave,
and my heart would find

345
00:24:49,930 --> 00:24:53,610
some ease from the evils this
worthless Nobody has brought me.'

346
00:24:53,610 --> 00:24:56,750
So speaking he sent the ram away from him, out the door.

347
00:24:56,750 --> 00:24:59,770
And when we had gone a little
way from the cave and the yard,

348
00:24:59,770 --> 00:25:04,490
first I loosed myself from under the ram,
and then set my companions free.

349
00:25:04,490 --> 00:25:08,510
Swiftly then we drove those
long-shanked sheep, deep in fat,

350
00:25:08,510 --> 00:25:11,170
turning again and again to look behind, until we came

351
00:25:11,170 --> 00:25:14,710
to the ship. And a welcome sight
we were to our dear companions —

352
00:25:14,710 --> 00:25:18,550
we who had escaped death —
but the others they wept and wailed for.

353
00:25:18,550 --> 00:25:21,930
I would not have it: I frowned each man's tears back

354
00:25:21,930 --> 00:25:24,290
and ordered them quickly to fling the fine-fleeced sheep

355
00:25:24,290 --> 00:25:27,590
aboard in numbers, and sail out over the salt water.

356
00:25:27,590 --> 00:25:31,210
And they came aboard at once and
took their places at the oarlocks,

357
00:25:31,210 --> 00:25:34,890
and sitting in order they struck
the gray salt water with their oars.

358
00:25:34,890 --> 00:25:41,080
But when I was as far offshore as a man's shout carries,
then I called to the Cyclops with jeering words:

359
00:25:41,080 --> 00:25:44,380
'Cyclops! Not so weak a man's companions, after all,

360
00:25:44,380 --> 00:25:47,590
were those you meant to eat by
main force in your hollow cave.

361
00:25:47,590 --> 00:25:50,350
And your own evil deeds were bound to find you out —

362
00:25:50,350 --> 00:25:53,090
merciless one — you who did not shrink from eating guests

363
00:25:53,090 --> 00:25:57,790
in your own house. For that Zeus
has paid you, and the other gods.'

364
00:25:57,790 --> 00:26:00,890
So I spoke, and the anger in his heart grew fiercer,

365
00:26:00,890 --> 00:26:04,170
and he tore off the peak of a
great mountain and hurled it,

366
00:26:04,170 --> 00:26:09,770
and brought it down just ahead of the dark-blue prow —
barely — it missed the tip of the steering-oar;

367
00:26:09,770 --> 00:26:15,210
and the sea heaved up under the falling rock, and the
backwash carried the ship straight in toward the land,

368
00:26:15,210 --> 00:26:18,430
a flood-surge out of the deep,
and drove her toward the shore.

369
00:26:18,430 --> 00:26:24,130
But I seized a long pole in my hands and shoved
her off and out, and roused my companions,

370
00:26:24,130 --> 00:26:27,810
ordering them to fall to their
oars and get us clear of the evil,

371
00:26:27,810 --> 00:26:31,370
nodding hard with my head; and they bent to it and rowed.

372
00:26:31,370 --> 00:26:34,130
But when we were twice as far out over the sea,

373
00:26:34,130 --> 00:26:38,030
then I made to call to the Cyclops
again; and around me my companions

374
00:26:38,030 --> 00:26:42,010
tried to hold me back with honeyed
words, this one and that one:

375
00:26:42,010 --> 00:26:44,650
'Merciless man — why must you provoke a savage

376
00:26:44,650 --> 00:26:48,290
who even now, with that shot
into the sea, has driven the ship

377
00:26:48,290 --> 00:26:51,550
straight back ashore — and we thought we died there!

378
00:26:51,550 --> 00:26:54,470
If he had heard a voice, heard any one of us speaking,

379
00:26:54,470 --> 00:26:57,430
he would have smashed our heads
and the ship's timbers together

380
00:26:57,430 --> 00:27:00,710
with one jagged boulder: he throws that far.'

381
00:27:00,710 --> 00:27:06,930
So they spoke, but they could not persuade my great heart,
and I called back to him with fury in my breast:

382
00:27:06,930 --> 00:27:12,670
'Cyclops — if any mortal man ever asks you
who put the shameful blinding on your eye,

383
00:27:12,670 --> 00:27:20,250
tell him: Odysseus, sacker of cities, blinded me —
the son of Laertes, who has his home in Ithaca.'

384
00:27:20,250 --> 00:27:23,770
So I spoke, and he groaned aloud, and answered:

385
00:27:23,770 --> 00:27:26,890
'Ah, then it comes home to me — the old prophecy!

386
00:27:26,890 --> 00:27:33,850
There was a seer here once, a great man and a good one,
Telemus, Eurymus' son, who excelled in prophecy

387
00:27:33,850 --> 00:27:35,870
and grew old prophesying among the Cyclopes.

388
00:27:35,870 --> 00:27:39,650
He told me all this would come to pass in time:

389
00:27:39,650 --> 00:27:43,110
that I would lose my sight at the hands of an Odysseus.

390
00:27:43,110 --> 00:27:49,030
But I always watched for some big man, a handsome one,
to come here, clothed in great strength;

391
00:27:49,030 --> 00:27:55,470
and now a little man, a worthless thing, a weakling,
has put out my eye — after he crushed me with wine.

392
00:27:55,470 --> 00:27:59,230
But come here, Odysseus —
let me set out a guest-gift for you,

393
00:27:59,230 --> 00:28:02,430
and press the famous earth-shaker
to give you passage home.

394
00:28:02,430 --> 00:28:05,870
For I am his son, and he declares himself my father.

395
00:28:05,870 --> 00:28:12,910
He himself, if he wills it, will heal me — and no one else,
none of the blessed gods, and no mortal man.'

396
00:28:12,910 --> 00:28:15,870
So he spoke; and I answered him and said:

397
00:28:15,870 --> 00:28:20,190
'Would to god I could strip you of breath and
life and send you down to the house of Hades

398
00:28:20,190 --> 00:28:24,130
as surely as not even the
earth-shaker will ever heal that eye.'

399
00:28:24,130 --> 00:28:30,010
So I spoke; and he then prayed to the lord Poseidon,
stretching out his hands to the starry heaven:

400
00:28:30,010 --> 00:28:33,450
'Hear me, Poseidon who hold the earth, dark-haired one:

401
00:28:33,450 --> 00:28:37,190
if I am truly yours, and you declare yourself my father,

402
00:28:37,190 --> 00:28:43,650
grant that Odysseus, sacker of cities, never come home —
the son of Laertes, who has his home in Ithaca.

403
00:28:43,650 --> 00:28:49,470
But if it is his portion to see his own people,
to reach his well-built house and his own fatherland,

404
00:28:49,470 --> 00:28:52,970
late may he come, and in evil case, all companions lost,

405
00:28:52,970 --> 00:28:56,270
on another man's ship — and find trouble in his house.'

406
00:28:56,270 --> 00:28:59,750
So he spoke in prayer, and the dark-haired god heard him.

407
00:28:59,750 --> 00:29:02,730
Then once again he lifted a stone — far bigger —

408
00:29:02,730 --> 00:29:06,930
whirled it, and let it fly, putting
his measureless strength behind it,

409
00:29:06,930 --> 00:29:12,650
and brought it down just astern of the dark-blue ship —
barely — it missed the tip of the steering-oar;

410
00:29:12,650 --> 00:29:15,010
and the sea heaved up under the falling rock,

411
00:29:15,010 --> 00:29:19,070
and the wave carried her forward,
and drove her toward the far shore.

412
00:29:19,070 --> 00:29:21,510
But when we came back to the island where the other

413
00:29:21,510 --> 00:29:24,410
well-benched ships waited all
together, with the companions

414
00:29:24,410 --> 00:29:29,970
sitting round them weeping, watching for us without end,
there we ran the ship up on the sands

415
00:29:29,970 --> 00:29:33,130
and stepped out ourselves onto the surf-line of the sea.

416
00:29:33,130 --> 00:29:36,250
And taking the Cyclops' flocks out of the hollow ship

417
00:29:36,250 --> 00:29:40,950
we divided all, so that no man
might go cheated of his share by me.

418
00:29:40,950 --> 00:29:44,210
But the great ram — my strong-greaved companions gave him

419
00:29:44,210 --> 00:29:48,430
to me alone, when the flocks were shared,
a gift apart; and on the shore

420
00:29:48,430 --> 00:29:52,190
I offered him to Zeus of the
black cloud, the son of Cronos,

421
00:29:52,190 --> 00:29:56,230
lord of all, and burned the thigh-cuts.
But he took no heed of my offering:

422
00:29:56,230 --> 00:30:00,070
he was pondering, even then, how all my well-benched ships

423
00:30:00,070 --> 00:30:03,710
might be destroyed, and all my trusted companions.

424
00:30:03,710 --> 00:30:10,310
So then all day long till the setting of the sun we
sat feasting on meat past counting and sweet wine;

425
00:30:10,310 --> 00:30:16,190
and when the sun went down and the dusk came on,
then we lay down to sleep on the surf-line of the sea.

426
00:30:16,190 --> 00:30:22,030
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,
then I roused my companions and ordered them

427
00:30:22,030 --> 00:30:24,710
to board in their turn and loose the stern-cables.

428
00:30:24,710 --> 00:30:29,070
And they came aboard at once and
took their places at the oarlocks,

429
00:30:29,070 --> 00:30:32,830
and sitting in order they struck
the gray salt water with their oars.

430
00:30:32,830 --> 00:30:39,506
From there we sailed on further, grieved at heart,
glad to be out of death, having lost our dear companions.
